<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fidesz</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Fidesz – Hungarian Civic Alliance</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fidesz is a Hungarian political party founded in 1988 as a liberal alliance of young democrats, which under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viktor-orban">Viktor Orbán</a> moved to national <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a>. It governed from 1998 to 2002 and won a two-thirds majority in 2010, which it used to adopt a new constitution, the Fundamental Law of 2011. It repeated its two-thirds majorities in later elections.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fidesz.jpg" alt="Fidesz" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fidesz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fidesz_2015.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Fidesz was founded in the spring of 1988 as Fiatal Demokraták Szövetsége, the Alliance of Young Democrats, an underground liberal student movement opposed to the ruling Hungarian Socialist Workers' Party. Founding such a movement was semi-illegal at the time, so its founders risked their careers. Membership had an upper age limit of 35 years, abolished at the 1993 congress. In 1989 the movement won the Thorolf Rafto Memorial Prize, received by one of its leaders, Péter Molnár. During the transition to multiparty democracy in 1989 and 1990 it became a political party, and in the 1990 election it entered the National Assembly with about 6 percent of the vote, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viktor-orban">Viktor Orbán</a> at its head.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1992 Fidesz joined the Liberal International. At its 1993 congress it redefined itself from liberal to civic-centrist, which split the membership: Péter Molnár, Gábor Fodor and Klára Ungár left for the liberal Alliance of Free Democrats, and Orbán was elected chairman. After a disappointing result in 1994 the party grew more conservative, and in 1995 it renamed itself the Hungarian Civic Party and sought links with the national-conservative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-democratic-forum">Hungarian Democratic Forum</a>.</p><p>Fidesz first took power at the 1998 election, with Orbán as prime minister, in coalition with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-democratic-forum">Hungarian Democratic Forum</a> and the Independent Smallholders' Party. In 2000 it left the Liberal International and joined the European People's Party. It narrowly lost the 2002 election to the Hungarian Socialist Party, with 41.07 percent against 42.05 percent, and accused its opponents of electoral fraud. In 2003 it took the name Fidesz – Hungarian Civic Union. In 2004 it won 47.4 percent of the vote in the European Parliament elections and 12 seats. Its nominee László Sólyom was elected President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 2005. During its years in opposition between 2002 and 2010 it formed an electoral alliance with the Christian Democratic People's Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Fidesz's position has shifted over time. It began on the centre-left, strongly committed to anti-clerical and secular policies, moved to the right from 1994, and by 1998 was the most prominent liberal-conservative force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, adopting nationalism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-democracy">Christian democracy</a> in the early 2000s. After winning a parliamentary supermajority in the 2010 election it promulgated a new constitution, the Fundamental Law, in 2011, and it won supermajorities again in 2014, 2018 and 2022.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fidesz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 2006 protests followed Prime Minister Ferenc Gyurcsány's leaked admission that his party had lied to win the election, and in 2010 Orbán's national-conservative <b>Fidesz</b> took a supermajority, repeated in 2014, 2018 and 2022.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…democratic status of some parties is also disputed: Turkey's Justice and Development Party moved towards the tradition, but the link is questioned, and Hungary's <b>Fidesz</b> was part of the European People's Party from 2004 to 2021, while its leader Viktor Orbán claimed Hungary to be a Christian democracy.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/christian-democracy">Christian democracy · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
